<\body>
  <doc-data|<doc-title|A Constructive Introduction to Quantum Field
  Theory>|<doc-subtitle|Lecture 4: The Polchinski flow and forward\Ubackward
  equations>|<doc-author|<author-data|<author-name|M.
  Gubinelli>|<author-affiliation|Mathematical Institute, University of
  Oxford>>>|<doc-date|November 2026 <emdash> <em|provisional>>>

  <abstract-data|<\abstract>
    Building on the scale decomposition and the stochastic observables of
    Lecture 3, we introduce interactions through the Polchinski equation for
    the effective potential. Its probabilistic counterpart is a
    forward\Ubackward stochastic differential equation (FBSDE) for the field
    seen at increasing resolution, whose effective force is a martingale.
    Writing the FBSDE in the coordinate given by the conditional mean of the
    terminal field removes its drift and integrates the transport part of the
    force flow exactly. In the gradient case the remaining equation for the
    force is the Wetterich equation for the effective average action, whose
    propagator is the conditional covariance. We then discuss the variational
    structure of the flow (Boué\UDupuis formula and Hamilton\UJacobi\UBellman
    equation) in finite and infinite volume. The additional material covers
    the algebraic and perturbative structure of the flow (semigroup,
    flatness, counterterms for <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>), and the
    Dyson\USchwinger equations at every scale. The treatment is formal: we
    compute as if all objects were smooth and integrable, and we indicate
    where the real difficulties lie.
  </abstract>>

